/// Behavioral API documentation for the viral trading content backend.
///
/// Static Markdown authored from the current backend source. It reads no state
/// and returns a constant document.
mixin () {
  public query func getApiDoc() : async Text {
    "# Viral Trading Content API\n\n## Purpose\n\nThis backend aggregates currently-viral trading and market content from multiple public, keyless sources and serves it to the reading-focused frontend. It stores normalized content items and exposes read endpoints plus a refresh endpoint that pulls fresh data.\n\n## Public methods\n\n- `listViralContent(filter : ContentFilter) : async [ContentItem]` — query. Returns stored content matching the optional filters, sorted by `viralityScore` descending (ties broken by newest `publishedAt` first). `filter.keyword` is a case-insensitive substring match against `title` and `topics`; `filter.source` is an exact match against `sourceName`; `filter.timeRange` is an optional inclusive range over `publishedAt`.\n- `getContent(id : ContentId) : async ?ContentItem` — query. Returns the item with the given id, or `null` if absent.\n- `getRelatedContent(id : ContentId) : async [ContentItem]` — query. Returns other items sharing at least one topic with the given item, sorted by `viralityScore` descending. Returns `[]` when the id is unknown or the item has no topics.\n- `listSources() : async [SourceDescriptor]` — query. Returns the fixed set of external sources this app aggregates.\n- `refreshContent() : async Nat` — update. Fetches fresh content from every external source, merges it into the store without duplicating existing items, and returns the number of newly added items.\n\n## Authentication and authorization\n\nAll methods are callable by any caller, including anonymous ones. This app does not gate reads or the refresh behind sign-in, and it does not use per-user ownership: the content is a shared, public aggregation. The `refreshContent` update is likewise open; it performs only bounded, keyless HTTP GET requests to public endpoints and cannot be used to spend cycles on behalf of a caller.\n\nBecause no endpoint requires a signed caller, there is no registration prerequisite and no role-guarded call. A caller that never signed in through the app frontend can still call every method.\n\n## Identity derivation\n\nThe app frontend pins an Internet Identity derivation origin, published at `/.well-known/ii-derivation-origin` when available. An agent already holding the user's Internet Identity authorization derives the correct per-app principal against that origin (for example `icp identity link web <name> --app <host>`). Such a delegation acts with the user's full authority in this app until it expires. This backend does not use the caller principal for authorization, so identity derivation does not change what a caller may do here.\n\n## Units and encoding\n\n- `ContentId` is a `Nat`.\n- `Timestamp` (`publishedAt`) is nanoseconds since the Unix epoch, as a signed `Int`. It may be negative for pre-1970 dates. The frontend is responsible for formatting it for display.\n- `viralityScore` is a non-negative `Nat`; higher means more viral. It is derived from source engagement signals plus a recency bonus and is not a normalized percentage.\n- `topics` is an array of short display tags (for example `Bitcoin`, `Macro`, `Stocks`).\n- `ContentFilter` fields are all optional; `null` means \"no constraint\".\n- `SourceDescriptor` carries a display `name`, a `url`, and a human-readable `description`.\n\n## Lifecycle and polling\n\nThe store starts empty. Content appears only after a successful `refreshContent` call. `refreshContent` is a single update that awaits three sequential HTTP outcalls (Crypto News, Hacker News, CoinGecko); it can take several seconds. A source that fails or returns a malformed payload contributes no items rather than failing the whole refresh, so a refresh can legitimately return `0`.\n\nClients should call `refreshContent` on demand (for example a manual refresh button or an initial load), then read via the query endpoints. There is no background timer and no push notification. Polling is safe but unnecessary; re-read after a refresh completes.\n\n## Mutation retry safety\n\n`refreshContent` is idempotent with respect to stored data: items are deduplicated by article URL (falling back to source name plus title), so re-running it does not create duplicate rows. It is safe to retry after a timeout. The returned count reflects only items newly added by that call, so a retry that adds nothing returns `0`. The update has no destructive effect and never deletes existing content.\n\n## Errors, traps, and gotchas\n\n- Query endpoints do not trap on missing data; `getContent` returns `null` and `getRelatedContent` returns `[]`.\n- `refreshContent` does not trap on a failing source; it silently contributes fewer items. A caller cannot distinguish \"no new content\" from \"all sources failed\" by the return value alone.\n- `publishedAt` for CoinGecko items is the time of the refresh, not a publication time, because the markets endpoint carries no timestamp.\n- `viralityScore` values are only comparable within this app's own scoring scheme; they are not comparable across sources in any absolute sense.\n- The OQL `schema()` and `execute()` endpoints expose the stored content collection to the Caffeine Data Intelligence agent under controller-only authorization; they are not part of the user-facing API.\n";
  };
};
